  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Golden Chanterelle is a highly prized edible mushroom with a distinctive funnel or trumpet shape and a golden-yellow color. It is one of the most sought-after wild mushrooms in New England and has been used in culinary traditions for centuries.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "False Gills (Ridges)",
      "description": "Features blunt, forked ridges running down the stem rather than true knife-like gills; these ridges are often the same color as the cap.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Color and Shape",
      "description": "Displays a bright golden-yellow to orange color with a funnel or trumpet-like shape that is wider at the top and narrows toward the base.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Firm, Pale Flesh",
      "description": "Has dense, firm flesh that is pale yellow inside and does not bruise or stain easily when handled.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Golden Chanterelles thrive in well-drained, mixed forests with oak, beech, and hemlock trees, typically appearing from summer through fall in New England woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Golden Chanterelles have a subtle fruity aroma and are sometimes called 'the fox of mushrooms' because they are notoriously difficult to cultivate commercially, making wild-harvested specimens especially valuable to foragers and chefs.",
